Maven – one of the most widely used build & project management tools in the DevOps world.
It plays a crucial role in transforming raw source code into a deployable artifact (.jar / .war / .ear).

💡 Key Takeaways:

Artifact → The final product of the code (e.g., .jar, .war, .ear).

Maven → A free, open-source, platform-independent build & project management tool written in Java.

POM.xml → The heart of Maven projects, containing all project details (dependencies, plugins, versioning, etc.).

Maven Lifecycle & Goals:

mvn compile → Compile code

mvn test → Run tests

mvn package → Create artifact

mvn install → Copy artifact to .m2 repo

mvn clean → Remove old builds



🔹 Why Maven?
Without Maven:
❌ No proper project structure
❌ No artifact creation
❌ Manual dependency management

With Maven:
✅ Automated builds & testing
✅ Easy dependency handling
✅ Lifecycle-driven process

⚡ Alternatives: Ant, Gradle, NPM

📌 Maven vs Ant:

Maven → Build + Project Management (Declarative, Lifecycle-based, POM.xml)

Ant → Only Build Tool (Procedural, Build.xml, No Lifecycle)


📝 In simple terms:
👉 Developers provide raw code + pom.xml.
👉 Maven builds, tests, and packages it into an artifact.
👉 That artifact is what we finally deploy 🚀
